Setting (from the previous and next pages). We start from an exact sequence of (constant, possibly pro-) groups 1 → M → 𝔊 → H → 1, with M abelian; this is the sequence (27). We want a group sheaf 𝔊̲ over the base S_0 that "linearizes" the kernel. Its sections over an affine S = Spec A should be the pushout 𝔊 ∧_M (M⊗_Z A). This is the extension of H by the vector group M⊗_Z A obtained from 𝔊 by pushing out along M → M⊗_Z A. The previous page builds it naively, as the Zariski sheafification of A ↦ 𝔊 ∧_M (M⊗A). It then announces a second presentation using the general construction of §49, carried out in the sheaf setting.

The input data (34) are:
- M̲ = M⊗_Z O, an abelian group sheaf on S_0;
- the constant group 𝔊_{S_0} with its normal subgroup M_{S_0};
- a homomorphism M_{S_0} → M̲;
- an action Θ of 𝔊_{S_0} on M̲, induced by functoriality from the action of 𝔊 on M by conjugation.
These must satisfy compatibilities a) and b). Presumably (a) Θ restricted to M_{S_0} is conjugation transported through M_{S_0} → M̲, and (b) M_{S_0} → M̲ is 𝔊-equivariant.

This page: §49 produces an extension (35), namely a group sheaf 𝔊̲ ⊃ M̲ (normal) together with Φ: 𝔊_{S_0} → 𝔊̲. One recovers (34) from (35) as follows:
- M_{S_0} = Φ^{-1}(M̲);
- the map M_{S_0} → M̲ is the one induced by Φ;
- Θ is the action through Φ followed by conjugation.
Moreover Φ induces an isomorphism 𝔊_{S_0}/M_{S_0} = H_{S_0} ≅ 𝔊̲/M̲. This gives an "axiomatic" (universal-property) description of extensions (35) in terms of the data (34). Concretely, 𝔊̲ = (M̲ ⋊ 𝔊_{S_0}) / {(f(m)^{-1}, m)}, the standard pushout of a group extension along an equivariant map of kernels.

One gets an exact sequence (36) of group sheaves 1 → M̲ → 𝔊̲ → H_{S_0} → 1. Passing to sections over an affine S = Spec A gives (37): 1 → M⊗_Z A → 𝔊̲(A) → H_{S_0}(S) → 1, with H_{S_0}(S) = H when S is connected. More generally this holds whenever H^1(S, M⊗_Z O_S) = 0. Right exactness holds because the fibre of 𝔊̲ → H_{S_0} over a section is an M̲-torsor, and on affines quasi-coherent H^1 vanishes.

The margin adds that the affine hypothesis actually serves only this purpose. Even more directly, the relevant torsors are trivial, since they come by pushout from the constant M-torsors given by the inverse images of sections of H in 𝔊. The next page then records the comparison diagram (38)/(39) of extensions: 𝔊 → 𝔊̲(A) over id_H, recovering iso (28) at S = S_0. It also records functoriality in the sequence (27).

Purpose: build a canonical "Lie/linearized" algebraic-group model of an extension of a discrete/profinite group, for the Galois–Teichmüller programme of La Longue Marche. Here 𝔊̲ is an extension of the constant group H by the vector group M⊗G_a, i.e. a relative linearization of 𝔊.
